## Paper excerpts (40 of 99, chosen for detail and spread over fields/years)

### 2016ApJ...823L..18H: Evidence for a CO Desorption Front in the Outer AS 209 Disk (2016; protoplanetary_disks, astrochemistry; observational_alma_primary)
- question: Does CO isotopologue emission in the AS 209 disk show CO enhancement (desorption) in the outer disk, beyond the expected CO snowline?
- step 3: Toy model of CO desorption ring and synthetic observations
  - method: Parametric density/temperature model with Gaussian ring in CO surface density; RADMC-3D line images; sampled at observed uv with VIS_SAMPLE; noise added; CLEANed in CASA; compared with and without ring [A toy model for CO desorption in the ou…: "After computing sky brightness images at the orientation of the AS 209 disk, model visibilities were produced at the same spatial frequencies as the AS 209 data by using Python package VIS_SAMPLE."]
  - software: RADMC-3D, VIS_SAMPLE, CASA 4.4.0
  - parameter: Sigma_c=5e-5 g/cm2, gamma=1, r_c=100 AU, B=3, r_ring=150 AU, sigma_ring=20 AU [A toy model for CO desorption in the ou…: "we set $\Sigma_c = 5\times 10^{-5} \text{ g cm}^{-2}$, $\gamma = 1$, $r_c = 100$ AU, $B = 3$, $r_\text{ring} = 150$ AU, and $\sigma_\text{ring} = 20$ AU."]
  - parameter: turbulent broadening xi = 0.01 km/s [A toy model for CO desorption in the ou…: "the turbulent broadening parameter was set to $\xi =$ 0.01 km s$^{-1}$"]
  - assumption: LTE; Keplerian velocities; ISM isotopologue ratios 69 and 557; T_atm,10=150 K, q_atm=0.5; C18O traces CO column [A toy model for CO desorption in the ou…: "Local thermal equilibrium was assumed because CO and its isotopologues have low critical densities relative to disk gas densities"]
  - validation: Qualitative morphology comparison with and without ring (B=0) [A toy model for CO desorption in the ou…: "only the model with an outer CO ring, shown in Figure [3](#fig3), reproduces the central peak and ring in the C$^{18}$O emission, and the shoulder in $^{13}$CO emission."]
- limitation (authors): Thermal vs non-thermal desorption contributions not constrained; narrow optically thick dust rings need higher resolution [Discussion: "Further observations and modeling are necessary to constrain the relative contributions of thermal versus non-thermal processes."]
- not stated (card): tclean/clean parameters: weighting/robust, cell size, image size, deconvolution threshold; Which SPWs were used for self-calibration and solution interval; Velocity range and channel width of final image cubes beyond 0.1 km/s; Details of the Keplerian mask parameters; Stellar mass/distance adopted in toy model beyond 0.9 Msun and 120 pc; grid setup and photon/radiative transfer settings in RADMC-…

### 2016A&A...587A..91B: Exploring molecular complexity with ALMA (EMoCA): Deuterated complex organic molecules in Sagittarius B2(N2) (2016; star_formation, astrochemistry, milky_way_ism; observational_alma_primary)
- question: Which deuterated complex organic molecules are detectable toward the hot core Sgr B2(N2), what are their deuteration levels, and how do they compare with astrochemical models and Orion KL?
- step 3: LTE modeling of full spectrum
  - method: Per-species synthetic LTE spectra with size, N, T, velocity offset and FWHM; four parameters optimized by eye; summed linearly into full model [3 Radiative transfer modeling: "The other four parameters were optimized by eye."]
  - software: Weeds
  - assumption: LTE, uniform physical structure, Gaussian source; deuterated species share parameters of main isotopologue [3 Radiative transfer modeling: "The physical structure of the source assumed for the modeling is uniform."]
  - fitting: Manual by-eye [3 Radiative transfer modeling of the li…: "The other four parameters were optimized by eye."]
  - validation: Detected if frequency, peak intensity and width are reproduced and line not contaminated [3 Radiative transfer modeling: "We emphasize that our definition of a “detected” line is very conservative"]
- step 4: Deuteration levels and upper limits
  - method: Column density of deuterated species from the main-species LTE model parameters; ratio XD/XH [5 Results: "This model is then used to obtain constraints on the column density of the deuterated species."]
  - software: Weeds
  - assumption: 12C/13C used to derive main species N from 13C isotopologues; statistical 2:1 distribution for out-of-plane/in-plane conformers [4 Spectroscopic predictions: "we assumed a statistical distribution (2:1) to compute and report individual column densities"]
  - validation: Compared with astrochemical models T14, A12 and Orion KL [abstract: "The results are compared to predictions of two astrochemical models that treat the deuteration process."]
- result: CH2DCN deuteration level = 0.4 ± not stated % [6.1.1: "we derive a deuterium fractionation of $0.4\%$ for this molecule"]
- result: CH2DOH deuteration = 0.12 ± not stated % [6.1.3: "The deuterium fractionation we derive for this isotopologue is 0.12%"]
- result: CH3OD deuteration upper limit = <0.07 ± not stated % [7 Conclusions: "CH$_3$OD ($<0.07\%$)"]
- result: DC3N deuteration = 0.09 ± not stated % [6.1.4: "We obtain a deuterium fractionation of 0.09%"]
- result: [CH2DOH]/[CH3OD] = >1.8 ± not stated dimensionless [6.3: "leads to \[CH$_2$DOH\]/\[CH$_3$OD\] $> 1.8$"]
- limitation (authors): Uniform single-component LTE model cannot fit very optically thick lines; formal temperature errors unreliable [5.2: "Here again, a more complex model with a non-uniform physical structure would be necessary to fit all transitions in a consistent way."]
- not stated (card): Exact line-free channel ranges per spectral window; Self-calibration solution intervals and the line used; Clean thresholds, masks, image size; Integration frequency boundaries for each line; Full list of identified transitions (stated as not available); Tolerances for by-eye LTE parameter optimization; Velocity of source systemic velocity assumed

### 2019A&A...624A.125M: Spatially resolved carbon and oxygen isotopic ratios in NGC 253 using optically thin tracers (2019; nearby_galaxies, astrochemistry; observational_alma_primary)
- question: What are the spatially resolved carbon and oxygen isotope ratios across the central molecular zone of the starburst NGC 253, derived from optically thin CO isotopologues?
- step 5: LTE column densities and ratios at 14 positions
  - method: Fit synthetic LTE spectra at selected C18O peak positions; velocity and width fixed to 13CO fit; average column density ratios with three weightings [3.2.2: "With MADCUBA we fit a synthetic spectrum, calculated from input physical parameters and using the molecular spectroscopic parameters in the JPL and CDMS catalogs, to the observed line profiles."]
  - software: MADCUBA
  - parameter: Tex = 15 K; source size 3'' [3.2.2: "ii)$ they have a common excitation temperature of $T_{ex}=15~$K, and $iii)$ the source size is $3''$"]
  - assumption: All isotopologues share distribution and velocity profile; column density ratios equal atomic isotopic ratios [3.2.2: "$i)$ all isotopologues have the same distribution, including velocity profile"]
  - validation: Tex 10 and 20 K change column densities by ~5% and ~10%; ratios nearly unchanged [3.2.2: "For temperatures of 10 and 20 K, we estimate column densities $\sim5\%$ and $\sim10\%$ higher, respectively."]
- result: 12C/13C (sigma-weighted, spectra) = 21 ± 6 dimensionless [Table 1: "$21\pm6$"]
- result: 16O/18O (sigma-weighted, spectra) = 130 ± 40 dimensionless [Table 1: "$130\pm40$"]
- result: 18O/17O (sigma-weighted, spectra) = 4.5 ± 0.8 dimensionless [Table 1: "$4.5\pm0.8$"]
- result: 13CO/C18O (sigma-weighted, spectra) = 6.1 ± 0.9 dimensionless [Table 1: "$6.1\pm0.9$"]
- limitation (authors): Fractionation not constrained; source size and Tex assumed; 13CO moderately optically thick; missing short spacings [Introduction: "Since we are not able to provide sensible constraints on fractionation issues in the framework of this paper"]
- not stated (card): tclean parameters (deconvolver, robust, masks, cell size, image size, threshold); line-free channel ranges for uvcontsub; whether pipeline or manual calibration was used; velocity range for map integration beyond 50-450 km/s; MADCUBA version and the fitting algorithm/priors; MADCUBA paper is in prep; how fit uncertainties on column densities were propagated

